></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="17:30 HKT · 42% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(56.25% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 26.688px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="18:00 HKT · 42% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(58.3333% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 26.688px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="18:30 HKT · 42% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(60.4167% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 26.688px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="19:00 HKT · 49% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(62.5% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 31.552px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="19:30 HKT · 51% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(64.5833% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 32.576px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="20:00 HKT · 54% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(66.6667% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 34.304px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="20:30 HKT · 67% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(68.75% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 43.136px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="21:00 HKT · 71% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(70.8333% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 45.248px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="21:30 HKT · 78% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(72.9167% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 49.984px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="22:00 HKT · 82% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(75% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 52.16px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="22:30 HKT · 82% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(77.0833% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 52.16px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="23:00 HKT · 85% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(79.1667% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 54.656px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="23:30 HKT · 91% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(81.25% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 58.048px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="00:00 HKT · 94% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(83.3333% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 60.352px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="00:30 HKT · 95% of the day's range by now, 19 days" style="left: calc(85.4167% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 60.8px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="01:00 HKT · 95% of the day's range by now, 19 days" style="left: calc(87.5% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 60.8px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="01:30 HKT · 100% of the day's range by now, 19 days" style="left: calc(89.5833% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 64px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="02:00 HKT · 100% of the day's range by now, 19 days" style="left: calc(91.6667% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 64px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="02:30 HKT · 100% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(93.75% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 64px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="03:00 HKT · 100% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(95.8333% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 64px;"></div><div class="seas-bar dim" title="03:30 HKT · 100% of the day's range by now, 20 days" style="left: calc(97.9167% + 1px); width: calc(2.08333% - 2px); height: 64px;"></div></div></div><div class="seas-axis"><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(1.04167%);">04:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(13.5417%);">07:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(26.0417%);">10:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(38.5417%);">13:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(51.0417%);">16:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(63.5417%);">19:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(76.0417%);">22:00</div><div class="seas-tick" style="left: calc(88.5417%);">01:00</div></div><div class="seas-mk" style="left: calc(48.9583%);"></div><div class="seas-mk-dot" style="left: calc(48.9583%);"></div></div>
    <div class="seas-key" id="seasKey">Clock is Hong Kong time — cash open <b>21:30</b>, cash close <b>04:00</b>. The vertical line is now. The gap on the right of the first band is the one-hour exchange halt, drawn rather than closed. <b>Grey bars rest on fewer than 21 days.</b><details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="key"><summary>Why the travel panel looks flat, and why some bars are grey</summary><div class="fwork-b"><b>The travel panel shares the volume panel’s scale on purpose.</b> Both are shares of the same day, and the whole point is that the open half hour is a modest bar in one and a tower in the other. Giving each its own height would hide exactly the thing worth seeing. The grey bars are the archive’s two edges: it starts and ends mid-session, so the buckets at either edge of the day have thinner evidence than the ones in the middle. Every bar carries its own count on hover, and the table view prints them all.</div></details></div>
    <div class="seas-sess" id="seasSess"><div class="seas-srow hd"><div>Session</div><div class="seas-sval">Travel</div><div class="seas-sval">Range÷travel</div></div><div class="seas-srow"><div class="seas-sname">Asia<i>&nbsp;</i></div><div class="seas-sval">25.6%</div><div class="seas-sval">0.092</div></div><div class="seas-srow"><div class="seas-sname">London<i>best converter on 9 of 21</i></div><div class="seas-sval">26.6%</div><div class="seas-sval">0.126</div></div><div class="seas-srow"><div class="seas-sname">New York<i>biggest mover on 20 of 21 days</i></div><div class="seas-sval">47.8%</div><div class="seas-sval">0.135</div></div><div class="subnote">Both columns are medians over <b>21 days</b>. Travel is each session’s share of the day’s total movement and the three sum to 100%.<details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="sess"><summary>What range÷travel measures, and why a median of 21 is not a number to take on trust</summary><div class="fwork-b">Range÷travel is how much of that movement turned into displacement — a pure number, so the two contracts are directly comparable. The disclosure at the foot of the card prints every day separately, because a median of 21 is not a number to take on trust.</div></details></div></div>
    <div id="seasLm"><div class="seas-lm"><div class="seas-lmt">13:30Z<i>21:30 HKT</i></div><div><b>The cash open is the day’s biggest half hour, and it is not close.</b> It carried <b>6.4%</b> of the day’s travel and <b>12.4%</b> of its volume. Ranked against all 46 half hours it came <b>first on 16 of 20 days</b> and was <b>never worse than second</b>.<details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="lm-open"><summary>Which of those two numbers to lean on</summary><div class="fwork-b">The rank is the claim worth keeping: first-on-16-of-20 is a coin flip away from a different fraction, never-worse-than-second-of-46 is not.</div></details></div></div><div class="seas-lm"><div class="seas-lmt">19:30Z<i>03:30 HKT</i></div><div><b>Volume and movement come apart at the close.</b> The half hour into the cash close takes <b>12.1%</b> of the day’s volume — second only to the open — on <b>3.6%</b> of its travel, and it was the day’s biggest half hour <b>not once</b>, median rank 6 of 46.<details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="lm-close"><summary>Why that gap is the expensive one</summary><div class="fwork-b">Size crossing is not the same event as price moving, and this is the half hour where reading one as the other costs the most.</div></details></div></div></div>
    <div class="advis" id="seasAdv"><span class="advis-hdr">Where today sits in the shape</span><div class="sday-hd"><span class="sday-pill odd">SHAPING WIDE</span><span class="sday-say">ES has covered <b>51.0 pt</b> and a typical day is <b>38%</b> through its range by this half hour, which projects <b>133.9</b> against a <b>59.3</b> median — <b>+126%</b>.</span></div><div class="sday"><div class="sday-track"><span class="sday-lab" style="left:32.92%;transform:translateX(-50%)">median 59.3</span><span class="sday-line"></span><span class="sday-done" style="width:28.33%" title="on the board: 51.0 pt"></span><span class="sday-cap" style="left:28.33%"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:70.56%" title="a closed day: 127.0 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:63.06%" title="a closed day: 113.5 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:52.78%" title="a closed day: 95.0 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:89.31%" title="a closed day: 160.8 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:41.39%" title="a closed day: 74.5 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:25.83%" title="a closed day: 46.5 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:34.03%" title="a closed day: 61.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:19.44%" title="a closed day: 35.0 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:32.22%" title="a closed day: 58.0 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:26.25%" title="a closed day: 47.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:41.25%" title="a closed day: 74.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:19.58%" title="a closed day: 35.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:32.36%" title="a closed day: 58.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:33.47%" title="a closed day: 60.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:36.94%" title="a closed day: 66.5 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:48.61%" title="a closed day: 87.5 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:31.25%" title="a closed day: 56.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:26.81%" title="a closed day: 48.3 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:29.86%" title="a closed day: 53.8 pt"></span><span class="sday-t" style="left:19.31%" title="a closed day: 34.8 pt"></span><span class="sday-med" style="left:32.92%"></span><span class="sday-proj" style="left:74.37%" title="projected full day: 133.9 pt">◆</span></div><div class="sday-ax"><span class="l">0</span><span class="r">180.0 pt</span></div><div class="sday-lg"><b>Bar</b> range so far · <b>◆</b> projected full day · <b>|</b> each of the 20 closed days</div></div><details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="adv"><summary>How that projection is built, and the two different medians under it</summary><div class="fwork-b">High 7,741.3 to low 7,690.3 since the cash close. The projection is an <b>estimate</b>, and it leans on two different medians rather than one, which the first draft of this sentence ran together into a single count that matched neither. The fraction it divides by is the middle of <b>21</b> days at this half hour. The 59.3 it is then measured against is the middle of <b>20</b> whole days that ran 127.0, 113.5, 95.0, 160.8, 74.5, 46.5, 61.3, 35.0, 58.0, 47.3, 74.3, 35.3, 58.3, 60.3, 66.5, 87.5, 56.3, 48.3, 53.8, 34.8 — printed rather than hidden, because a median of 20 is a weak thing to divide a trading day by and the reader is entitled to see how weak.</div></details><div class="sday-prep"><b>Preparing from this:</b> The New York cash session has not started. <b>46%</b> of a typical day’s travel sits in that window, out of <b>67%</b> left in the day as a whole. Most of what builds today’s range has not happened yet, and a level that has held through Asia and London has not yet met the session that breaks them. This card says nothing about direction.<details class="fwork seas-f" data-t="adv-prep"><summary>Why New York is the session this hangs on, and where direction is asked</summary><div class="fwork-b">New York is the session that turns motion into displacement on this contract — 0.135 of range per unit of travel against Asia’s 0.092, and the best of the three on 7 of 21 days. Direction is the killzone card’s question, and it is asked and refused there rather than answered here.</div></details></div></div>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="seasSum">How this is measured, and the three things it refuses to tell you</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="seasBody"><p><b>What is counted.</b> Every five-minute bar in the relay’s archive is dropped into the half hour it belongs to. <b>Travel</b> sums the bars’ own high-lows, which is additive and therefore partitions a day exactly — within any single day the buckets sum to 100%. The figure published per bucket is the <b>median across days</b>, and medians do not add: the middle day of one half hour is rarely the middle day of the next, so the column you see sums to roughly 108%, not 100. Nothing on this card quotes that sum as a whole; where a run of buckets is reported as a share — “so much travel still ahead” — it is divided by the column’s own total rather than by 100. <b>Range</b> is high minus low over a span, which is not additive either: a session can travel enormously and finish where it started, and that gap is the entire subject of the range÷travel column. Volume is the same bars’ volume, as a share of the day. Nothing here is a price, so nothing here goes stale when the market moves; it goes stale when the archive grows, and the header on the block carries the window it was measured over.</p><p><b>Which days count, and it is two different answers.</b> The bucket table needs a day whose total travel is a day’s travel, so a day-label is measured only if it traded all three sessions. That test is not cosmetic: the archive’s day begins at the New York cash close, which peels the hour of trade after a Friday off into a label of its own — twelve bars, two buckets, and inside that label those two buckets are half the day’s travel each and 100% of its range. The day-range median needs something stricter still, because the projection above divides by it: only a <b>finished</b> day has a whole day’s range, and the newest label in the archive is the one being published into right now. So the buckets are measured on <b>21 days</b> and the day-range median on <b>20</b>, and the two numbers are different on purpose.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left">Day</th><th>Asia trav</th><th>Lon trav</th><th>NY trav</th><th>Asia eff</th><th>Lon eff</th><th>NY eff</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 1</td><td>29.3%</td><td>34.0%</td><td>36.8%</td><td>0.091</td><td>0.146</td><td>0.169</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 2</td><td>20.0%</td><td>21.3%</td><td>58.8%</td><td>0.151</td><td>0.126</td><td>0.135</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 3</td><td>29.3%</td><td>30.5%</td><td>40.3%</td><td>0.089</td><td>0.084</td><td>0.262</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 4</td><td>23.6%</td><td>23.8%</td><td>52.6%</td><td>0.091</td><td>0.146</td><td>0.310</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 5</td><td>21.1%</td><td>21.1%</td><td>57.8%</td><td>0.117</td><td>0.121</td><td>0.110</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 6</td><td>25.6%</td><td>22.8%</td><td>51.6%</td><td>0.078</td><td>0.077</td><td>0.075</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 7</td><td>27.0%</td><td>23.4%</td><td>49.6%</td><td>0.058</td><td>0.169</td><td>0.110</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 8</td><td>27.8%</td><td>24.6%</td><td>47.6%</td><td>0.137</td><td>0.138</td><td>0.090</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 9</td><td>24.9%</td><td>27.3%</td><td>47.8%</td><td>0.113</td><td>0.116</td><td>0.139</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 10</td><td>21.1%</td><td>28.8%</td><td>50.2%</td><td>0.067</td><td>0.160</td><td>0.118</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 11</td><td>23.8%</td><td>21.8%</td><td>54.4%</td><td>0.092</td><td>0.120</td><td>0.178</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 12</td><td>22.6%</td><td>23.1%</td><td>54.2%</td><td>0.064</td><td>0.086</td><td>0.107</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 13</td><td>20.3%</td><td>28.9%</td><td>50.8%</td><td>0.116</td><td>0.134</td><td>0.142</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 14</td><td>28.0%</td><td>28.3%</td><td>43.8%</td><td>0.173</td><td>0.099</td><td>0.148</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 15</td><td>26.9%</td><td>23.9%</td><td>49.2%</td><td>0.069</td><td>0.197</td><td>0.105</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 16</td><td>23.7%</td><td>29.4%</td><td>46.9%</td><td>0.057</td><td>0.191</td><td>0.141</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 17</td><td>27.0%</td><td>26.6%</td><td>46.4%</td><td>0.058</td><td>0.142</td><td>0.135</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 18</td><td>31.4%</td><td>27.7%</td><td>40.9%</td><td>0.112</td><td>0.082</td><td>0.080</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 19</td><td>29.3%</td><td>30.1%</td><td>40.7%</td><td>0.115</td><td>0.122</td><td>0.103</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 20</td><td>25.0%</td><td>29.4%</td><td>45.6%</td><td>0.107</td><td>0.086</td><td>0.087</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left">day 21</td><td>72.8%</td><td>7.1%</td><td>20.1%</td><td>0.122</td><td>0.309</td><td>0.257</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Read down the last three columns rather than across the medians: New York converts motion into displacement better than Asia on <b>7 of 21</b> days and carries the most travel on <b>20 of 21</b>, and unanimity on a five-day sample is worth more than the size of any one median.</p><p><b>The cumulative curve is not guaranteed to rise.</b> Each figure is the median of that bucket over the days that reached it, and different buckets were reached by different subsets of days, so two neighbours can be measured on different evidence. That is why the third panel is columns rather than a line — a line would draw an interpolation between two numbers that were never measured together — and why every bar carries its own day count on hover and in the table.</p><p><b>Three things this refuses to publish.</b> First, any day-of-week or month-of-year statistic. The archive holds <b>21 trading days</b>, which is one observation of each weekday and none of any month; a weekday table built on that would be five numbers with an n of one apiece, and the fact that it would look exactly like the tables above is what makes it dangerous. It becomes measurable at <b>20</b> trading days — <b>now</b>. Second, any direction rate: 13 of the 21 days closed up, so up-day share, close-above-open and high-made-in-session are all that one fact wearing different hats. Magnitude does not have that problem, which is why this card measures magnitude. Third, a continuation-versus-reversal rate — and this card could have produced one by a back door, from the first New York sweep of the London range. It measured ES continuing 2 of 5 and RTY 4 of 5. Those disagree with each other, are one sample of five apiece, and would have sat on the same page as a killzone card that says in its own words that the direction question is not yet answerable. One page does not get two answers to one question depending on which card you read.</p><p><b>Two contracts, and only two.</b> The relay banks five-minute bars for ES and RTY. Everything else on this page arrives as a six-minute quote sample, which is coarser than the half hour this card counts in — a “travel” figure built from it would be reporting the sampler’s step size as the market’s movement. That is the same reason the depth board below has no HSI panel.</p><p class="subnote">Window 2026-07-30 to 2026-08-27 — 25 day-labels, 21 of which traded all three sessions. Generated by seasgen.py and swapped in by refresh.sh; never edited by hand.</p></div>
    </details>
