<!-- SESSION LIQUIDITY -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,RTY,NQ,YM" id="rail-session-liquidity">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Session liquidity — what is still on the table</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>The high and the low of each killzone that has closed, and whether price has been back through them yet. </summary><div class="fwork-b">A level still standing is where resting orders still are; one already run is history. Distances are in points and in typical thirty-minute moves for the hour you are actually in.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <!-- C1: the card's promoted row. Painted by the SAME pass that computes
         ups/dns for the reach strip below — one derivation, two renderings,
         never a second hand copy (the count-vs-rows lesson). -->
    <div id="kzKey"><div class="kz-key"><span class="kz-key-lab">First in line</span><span class="kz-key-side">▲ NQ 29,654.75 (Asia high, 3.2×)</span><span class="kz-key-side">▼ RTY 3,005.70 (Asia low, 0.4×)</span></div></div>
    <div id="kzHost"><div class="kz-sym"><div class="kz-head"><span class="kz-name">ES</span><span class="kz-px">7,717.00</span><span class="kz-path">5m bars</span><span class="kz-path">typical 30m 6.75</span></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> high · set 06:35 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,741.25</div><div class="kz-dist">24.25 · 3.6×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> high · set 15:30 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,722.00</div><div class="kz-dist">5.00 · 0.7×</div></div><div class="kz-now"><div class="kz-arrow">▶</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>price now</b></div><div class="kz-val">7,717.00</div><div class="kz-dist"></div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> low · set 15:00 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,713.50</div><div class="kz-dist">3.50 · 0.5×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> high · set 04:55 <span class="kz-chip taken">run 06:00</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,710.25</div><div class="kz-dist">6.75 · 1.0×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> low · set 14:10 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,707.00</div><div class="kz-dist">10.00 · 1.5×</div></div><div class="kz-row far"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> low · set 00:40 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">7,671.00</div><div class="kz-dist">46.00 · 6.8×</div></div></div><div class="kz-sym"><div class="kz-head"><span class="kz-name">NQ</span><span class="kz-px">29,502.75</span><span class="kz-path">5m bars</span><span class="kz-path">typical 30m 48.00</span></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> high · set 08:00 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,654.75</div><div class="kz-dist">152.00 · 3.2×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> high · set 15:25 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,548.75</div><div class="kz-dist">46.00 · 1.0×</div></div><div class="kz-now"><div class="kz-arrow">▶</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>price now</b></div><div class="kz-val">29,502.75</div><div class="kz-dist"></div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> low · set 15:00 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,477.25</div><div class="kz-dist">25.50 · 0.5×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> high · set 04:55 <span class="kz-chip taken">run 06:00</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,437.75</div><div class="kz-dist">65.00 · 1.4×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> low · set 14:10 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,401.75</div><div class="kz-dist">101.00 · 2.1×</div></div><div class="kz-row far"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> low · set 22:25 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">29,156.75</div><div class="kz-dist">346.00 · 7.2×</div></div></div><div class="kz-sym"><div class="kz-head"><span class="kz-name">RTY</span><span class="kz-px">3,007.40</span><span class="kz-path">5m bars</span><span class="kz-path">typical 30m 3.90</span></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> high · set 22:00 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,020.50</div><div class="kz-dist">13.10 · 3.4×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> high · set 06:40 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,020.10</div><div class="kz-dist">12.70 · 3.3×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> high · set 15:15 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,010.30</div><div class="kz-dist">2.90 · 0.7×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> low · set 15:35 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,007.40</div><div class="kz-dist">0.00 · 0.0×</div></div><div class="kz-now"><div class="kz-arrow">▶</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>price now</b></div><div class="kz-val">3,007.40</div><div class="kz-dist"></div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> low · set 14:10 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,005.70</div><div class="kz-dist">1.70 · 0.4×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> low · set 21:30 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">3,002.60</div><div class="kz-dist">4.80 · 1.2×</div></div></div><div class="kz-sym"><div class="kz-head"><span class="kz-name">YM</span><span class="kz-px">53,630</span><span class="kz-path">5m bars</span><span class="kz-path">typical 30m 48</span></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> high · set 06:35 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,811</div><div class="kz-dist">181 · 3.8×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> high · set 04:55 <span class="kz-chip taken">run 06:00</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,728</div><div class="kz-dist">98 · 2.0×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▲</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> high · set 15:00 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,662</div><div class="kz-dist">32 · 0.7×</div></div><div class="kz-now"><div class="kz-arrow">▶</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>price now</b></div><div class="kz-val">53,630</div><div class="kz-dist"></div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>London</b> low · set 15:35 <span class="kz-chip here">so far</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,620</div><div class="kz-dist">10 · 0.2×</div></div><div class="kz-row"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>Asia</b> low · set 14:10 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,602</div><div class="kz-dist">28 · 0.6×</div></div><div class="kz-row far"><div class="kz-arrow">▼</div><div class="kz-lab"><b>New York</b> low · set 00:40 <span class="kz-chip standing">standing</span></div><div class="kz-val">53,435</div><div class="kz-dist">195 · 4.1×</div></div></div></div>
    <!-- Which contracts on the relay this card does not carry, and why. The text
         is kzgen's, verbatim out of KZD.excl -- it is the sentence the exclusion
         was decided in, and a painter rewording it here would be a second copy
         of a judgement. -->
    <div class="kz-excl" id="kzExcl"><b>Not on this card:</b> HSI — Hong Kong's session is not a killzone of the US day, and its structure has its own board on the tape card. Putting it here would be a decision about meaning rather than about data availability: every level on this card is where one session left something for the next one, and HSI's day does not stand in that relation to the others.</div>
    <div class="advis" id="kzAdv"><span class="advis-hdr">How to read the day</span><div class="kzr"><div class="kzr-lane"><div class="kzr-sym">ES</div><div class="kzr-track"><span class="kzr-band" style="left:40.91%;width:18.18%"></span><span class="kzr-line"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:82.66%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:36.53%"></span><span class="kzr-now"></span><span class="kzr-off l">+1</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:36.53%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:36.53%;transform:translateX(-50%)">1.5×</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:82.66%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:82.66%;transform:translateX(-50%)">3.6×</span></div></div><div class="kzr-lane"><div class="kzr-sym">NQ</div><div class="kzr-track"><span class="kzr-band" style="left:40.91%;width:18.18%"></span><span class="kzr-line"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:78.79%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:30.87%"></span><span class="kzr-now"></span><span class="kzr-off l">+1</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:30.87%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:30.87%;transform:translateX(-50%)">2.1×</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:78.79%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:78.79%;transform:translateX(-50%)">3.2×</span></div></div><div class="kzr-lane"><div class="kzr-sym">RTY</div><div class="kzr-track"><span class="kzr-band" style="left:40.91%;width:18.18%"></span><span class="kzr-line"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:79.60%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:46.04%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:80.54%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:38.81%"></span><span class="kzr-now"></span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:46.04%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:46.04%;transform:translateX(-50%)">0.4×</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:79.60%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:79.60%;transform:translateX(-50%)">3.3×</span></div></div><div class="kzr-lane"><div class="kzr-sym">YM</div><div class="kzr-track"><span class="kzr-band" style="left:40.91%;width:18.18%"></span><span class="kzr-line"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:84.28%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:44.70%"></span><span class="kzr-t" style="left:13.07%"></span><span class="kzr-now"></span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:44.70%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:44.70%;transform:translateX(-50%)">0.6×</span><span class="kzr-m" style="left:84.28%">◆</span><span class="kzr-lab" style="left:84.28%;transform:translateX(-50%)">3.8×</span></div></div><div class="kzr-ax"><span style="left:0%">5.5× below</span><span style="left:50%">now</span><span style="left:100%;transform:translateX(-100%)">5.5× above</span></div><details class="fwork kz-adv kzr-legend" data-t="scale"><summary><b>◆</b> nearest untouched level each side · shaded band is one typical move · <b>+N</b> is levels further out</summary><div class="fwork-b">Distance is measured in <b>typical thirty-minute moves</b> for that contract, so a level on ES and a level on RTY can be compared by which one price reaches first rather than by how many points away it is. The band is drawn as a band and not a line because the number behind it is a median, not a boundary. <b>+N</b> at an edge counts levels beyond the axis; the furthest standing is 7.2×. They are counted rather than drawn at the edge, because a mark at the edge would claim a distance the level does not have. ES and RTY only — NQ and YM have no measured pace, and a second figure of the same name measured a different way is the bug the one-divisor rule exists to stop.</div></details></div><div class="kzr-hd"><span class="kzr-pill out">NEITHER SIDE CLOSE</span><span class="kzr-say">Neither pool is inside a typical move.</span><details class="fwork kz-adv" data-t="shape"><summary><b>Why that reading</b></summary><div class="fwork-b">Neither side is close enough to call a magnet. This is the in-between reading and it is reported as one rather than rounded into the nearest confident case.</div></details></div><p style="margin:0 0 9px">Now in the <b>London</b> killzone, until 21:30 HKT. Levels from the sessions before it are armed; London’s own high and low do not become levels until it closes.</p><p style="margin:0 0 9px"><b>Already run:</b> 3 session highs and 0 lows across the four contracts. All on the same side — four contracts clearing the same kind of level is one move seen four times, not four independent events, and it is the signature of a trend rather than of liquidity being picked off.</p><details class="fwork kz-adv" data-t="table"><summary><b>Behind the first in line</b> — the runner-up each side.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Covers ES, NQ, RTY, YM — every contract with a usable pace divisor. Next above after that: RTY 3,020.10 (Asia high, 3.3×), then RTY 3,020.50 (New York high, 3.4×). Next below after that: YM 53,602 (Asia low, 0.6×), then RTY 3,002.60 (New York low, 1.2×).</div></details><details class="fwork kz-adv" data-t="div"><summary><b>Divergence.</b> ES and NQ and YM took New York’s high; RTY did not.</summary><div class="fwork-b">One index making the sweep while its partner refuses is the classic SMT setup, and the textbook reads it as the sweep lacking conviction. This archive is five days old and has <i>not</i> measured whether it resolves that way, so it is flagged here and deliberately not scored. Treat it as a reason to want a second signal, not as one.</div></details><details class="fwork kz-adv" data-t="base"><summary>The base rate is the one thing here with enough behind it to lean on: <b>32 of 52</b> prior-session extremes (62%) were traded through within about thirty hours.</summary><div class="fwork-b">That argues for treating a standing level as a target rather than as support to lean on. It says nothing about which way price goes once the level is gone — see below for why that number is withheld and when it arrives.</div></details></div>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary id="kzSum"><span>Prior-session levels get taken more often than they hold — <b>260 of 428</b> (61%). Which way price goes afterwards is not yet answerable — open for the working, and the date it becomes one.</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="kzBody"><p>A <b>level</b> is the high or the low of a killzone that has <i>closed</i>. While a session is running its extreme is labelled “so far” above and is not counted: a session making a new high has swept nothing, and treating the two as the same thing is the difference between a liquidity event and a trend.</p><p><b>Whose levels get taken.</b> Counted on ES and RTY, where five-minute bars exist for all twenty-four hours.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Level set in</th><th>Taken</th><th>Tracked</th><th>Rate</th></tr><tr><td>Asia</td><td>102</td><td>144</td><td>71%</td></tr><tr><td>London</td><td>96</td><td>144</td><td>67%</td></tr><tr><td>New York</td><td>62</td><td>140</td><td>44%</td></tr><tr><td><b>All</b></td><td><b>260</b></td><td><b>428</b></td><td><b>61%</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The spread down that column is the part worth arguing about: Asia’s levels went 71% of the time and New York’s 44%, on 144 and 140 levels. A plausible reading is that a level set in thin hours is easier to run than one set with the cash session behind it. At these counts that is a hypothesis with a number attached, not a result — the difference is roughly what you would get from three coin flips landing the same way.</p><p><b>Does the sweep stick?</b> Rows are the killzone that did the taking. The question is whether price is still on the far side of the level a fixed number of hours later. Fixed elapsed hours, not “by the next New York close” — that first version gave an Asia sweep twenty hours to be judged and a late-NY sweep one, so its three rows were three different experiments printed in one table.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Taken during</th><th>+6h</th><th>+12h</th><th>+24h</th></tr><tr><td>Asia</td><td><span class="disc-yes">17/32</span></td><td><span class="disc-no">12/32</span></td><td><span class="disc-no">14/32</span></td></tr><tr><td>London</td><td><span class="disc-yes">65/113</span></td><td><span class="disc-yes">66/113</span></td><td><span class="disc-no">55/113</span></td></tr><tr><td>New York</td><td><span class="disc-no">52/115</span></td><td><span class="disc-no">54/115</span></td><td><span class="disc-no">45/115</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Green is “still beyond the level”, red is “back through it”, and the fraction is printed beside every one because <b>n is between 6 and 16</b>. Asia sweeps sticking more often than New York ones is the direction this leans; at these counts it is a lean and not a finding.</p><p><b>Why there is no continuation-vs-reversal percentage on this card.</b> There is a number and it is not stable. Below is the share of sweeps that reversed, recomputed at six displacement thresholds — the constant that decides how far price must travel before the move counts as going somewhere.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Asia</th><th>London</th><th>New York</th></tr><tr><td>0.5×</td><td>61% <span style="opacity:.6">(62/102)</span></td><td>54% <span style="opacity:.6">(52/96)</span></td><td>48% <span style="opacity:.6">(30/62)</span></td></tr><tr><td>0.75×</td><td>55% <span style="opacity:.6">(56/102)</span></td><td>58% <span style="opacity:.6">(56/96)</span></td><td>47% <span style="opacity:.6">(29/62)</span></td></tr><tr><td>1×</td><td>57% <span style="opacity:.6">(58/102)</span></td><td>68% <span style="opacity:.6">(65/96)</span></td><td>48% <span style="opacity:.6">(30/62)</span></td></tr><tr><td>1.5×</td><td>51% <span style="opacity:.6">(52/102)</span></td><td>64% <span style="opacity:.6">(61/96)</span></td><td>35% <span style="opacity:.6">(22/62)</span></td></tr><tr><td>2×</td><td>42% <span style="opacity:.6">(43/102)</span></td><td>57% <span style="opacity:.6">(55/96)</span></td><td>32% <span style="opacity:.6">(20/62)</span></td></tr><tr><td>3×</td><td>24% <span style="opacity:.6">(24/102)</span></td><td>34% <span style="opacity:.6">(33/96)</span></td><td>23% <span style="opacity:.6">(14/62)</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The London row crosses the coin flip on its way down that column. A number that changes sign when an arbitrary constant is doubled is a finding about the constant, not about the market, so it is withheld rather than shipped with a caveat. Only the New York column holds its shape across every threshold.</p><p><b>When this becomes answerable.</b> Separating a true 65% from a coin flip at the usual thresholds needs <b>90</b> sweeps. The archive holds <b>260</b> and adds about 9.2 a day. That date is arithmetic, printed so that “not enough data” is a thing with a deadline rather than a permanent excuse.</p><p><b>What is excluded, and why.</b> Cash indices — SPX, NDX, RUT, HSI — are shut for two of the three killzones, so their quote series is flat there and every sweep resolves instantly against a zero-width move. Run anyway, HSI reported 26 sweeps and 26 continuations. A 100% hit rate is not a discovery, it is the shape a divide-by-nothing makes. Only contracts that trade all three killzones are counted.</p><p><b>HSI instead.</b> It trades inside one killzone, so it cannot have a killzone board; the equivalent question is what the afternoon does with the morning’s range. Only 5 clean days — a description, not a base rate.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Day</th><th>AM high</th><th>AM low</th><th>PM broke</th><th>Closed beyond</th></tr><tr><td>2026-08-20</td><td>25,807.0</td><td>25,632.4</td><td>high</td><td><span class="disc-no">no</span></td></tr><tr><td>2026-08-21</td><td>25,911.6</td><td>25,807.6</td><td>high</td><td><span class="disc-yes">yes</span></td></tr><tr><td>2026-08-24</td><td>25,780.1</td><td>25,430.9</td><td>neither</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>2026-08-25</td><td>25,676.8</td><td>25,403.4</td><td>neither</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>2026-08-26</td><td>25,800.2</td><td>25,615.9</td><td>neither</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="margin:0"><b>Sources.</b> Levels and counts from the relay archive, 2026-07-30 to 2026-08-27 (21 trading days), regenerated every refresh by kzgen.py. Distances divide by the same session pace every other × on this page uses.</p></div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- OVERNIGHT RANGE MAP -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,RTY,NQ,YM,HSI" id="rail-overnight-range-map">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Overnight range map</div>
    <div class="card-sub">Where each contract sits inside the range it has built since the last US cash close. The delay costs the last tick; it does not touch the range — that took hours to make.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div id="rangeMap"><div class="rt"><div class="rt-head"><span class="rt-sym">ES</span><span class="rt-sub">E-mini S&amp;P 500</span><span class="rt-px">7,717.00</span></div><div class="rt-track"><span class="rt-line"></span><span class="rt-fill" style="width:52.5%"></span><span class="rt-prev" style="left:0%"></span><span class="rt-dot" style="left:52.5%"></span></div><div class="rt-ends"><span>7,690.25</span><span>7,741.25</span></div><div class="rt-pos"><b style="color:var(--up)">+0.35%</b> · 53% of the overnight range · quote 12m old</div></div><div class="rt"><div class="rt-head"><span class="rt-sym">RTY</span><span class="rt-sub">E-mini Russell 2000</span><span class="rt-px">3,007.40</span></div><div class="rt-track"><span class="rt-line"></span><span class="rt-fill" style="width:11.799999999999999%"></span><span class="rt-prev" style="left:30.555555555555998%"></span><span class="rt-dot" style="left:11.799999999999999%"></span></div><div class="rt-ends"><span>3,005.70</span><span>3,020.10</span></div><div class="rt-pos"><b style="color:var(--down)">-0.09%</b> · 12% of the overnight range · quote 12m old</div></div><div class="rt"><div class="rt-head"><span class="rt-sym">NQ</span><span class="rt-sub">E-mini Nasdaq 100</span><span class="rt-px">29,502.75</span></div><div class="rt-track"><span class="rt-line"></span><span class="rt-fill" style="width:59.099999999999994%"></span><span class="rt-prev" style="left:1.6823687752355316%"></span><span class="rt-dot" style="left:59.099999999999994%"></span></div><div class="rt-ends"><span>29,283.25</span><span>29,654.75</span></div><div class="rt-pos"><b style="color:var(--up)">+0.73%</b> · 59% of the overnight range · quote 12m old</div></div><div class="rt"><div class="rt-head"><span class="rt-sym">YM</span><span class="rt-sub">E-mini Dow</span><span class="rt-px">53,630.00</span></div><div class="rt-track"><span class="rt-line"></span><span class="rt-fill" style="width:35.4%"></span><span class="rt-prev" style="left:0%"></span><span class="rt-dot" style="left:35.4%"></span></div><div class="rt-ends"><span>53,531.00</span><span>53,811.00</span></div><div class="rt-pos"><b style="color:var(--up)">+0.20%</b> · 35% of the overnight range · quote 12m old</div></div><div class="rt"><div class="rt-head"><span class="rt-sym">MHI</span><span class="rt-sub">Mini Hang Seng Index · HK cash hours, so this is not the US overnight window the others share · no change figure: prev_close froze after Thursday, thawed wrong — this payload is on the yahoo path and still publishes 25858.88 against a true Friday close of 25884.43, so the defect outlived the vendor swap and is structural; freeze/thaw is unobservable from one payload</span><span class="rt-px">25,557.89</span></div><div class="rt-track"><span class="rt-line"></span><span class="rt-fill" style="width:19.6%"></span><span class="rt-dot" style="left:19.6%"></span></div><div class="rt-ends"><span>25,500.96</span><span>25,792.01</span></div><div class="rt-pos"><b style="color:var(--text-muted)">—</b> · 20% of the overnight range · quote 17m old</div></div></div>
    <div class="rt-key"><u></u>prior close · ● last price · the filled bar is the distance travelled up from the overnight low</div>
    <button class="mini" type="button" id="rangeTvBtn" style="margin-top:12px">Show table view</button>
    <div class="tvwrap" id="rangeTvWrap"><table class="tvbox" id="rangeTv"><tbody><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Last</th><th>Chg</th><th>ON low</th><th>ON high</th><th>Pos</th><th>Prior</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>7,717.00</td><td>+0.35%</td><td>7,690.25</td><td>7,741.25</td><td>53%</td><td>7,690.00</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>3,007.40</td><td>-0.09%</td><td>3,005.70</td><td>3,020.10</td><td>12%</td><td>3,010.10</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>29,502.75</td><td>+0.73%</td><td>29,283.25</td><td>29,654.75</td><td>59%</td><td>29,289.50</td></tr><tr><td>YM</td><td>53,630.00</td><td>+0.20%</td><td>53,531.00</td><td>53,811.00</td><td>35%</td><td>53,521.00</td></tr><tr><td>MHI</td><td>25,557.89</td><td>—</td><td>25,500.96</td><td>25,792.01</td><td>20%</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CROSS-MARKET AGREEMENT -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,NQ,RTY,YM" id="rail-cross-market-agreement">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Cross-market agreement</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>Whether the four index futures made the <b>same kind of swing at the same moment</b>. </summary><div class="fwork-b">When one refuses to follow the others to a new high or a new low, that is the divergence traders call SMT — and this card shows it, dates it, and then shows the 148-hour measurement that says <b>not to trade it</b>. Everything here is anchored on ES, because reading each contract’s own pivots would compare four different moments and report the timing difference as a disagreement.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div id="smtNow"><div class="smt-when smt-gen">measured 13 Aug 08:24 UTC · 13 days ago <span class="dim">as measured</span> <span class="dim">· carried forward: the last refresh could not regenerate this card</span></div><div class="smt-blk"><div class="smt-hd"><div class="smt-kind">Last swing high</div><span class="smt-when">13 Aug 01:00 UTC · 342.9h ago · ES 7770.25</span><span class="smt-chip one">ONE DIVERGED</span><span class="smt-chip gone">TRADED THROUGH</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>ES</b><span class="a-dir dn">▼</span><span class="a-note">lower high <span style="opacity:.7">(the anchor)</span></span><span class="a-val">−6.75</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>NQ</b><span class="a-dir dn">▼</span><span class="a-note">lower high</span><span class="a-val">−72.25</span></div><div class="smt-arm odd"><b>RTY</b><span class="a-dir up">▲</span><span class="a-note">higher high — the odd one out</span><span class="a-val">+7.10</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>YM</b><span class="a-dir dn">▼</span><span class="a-note">lower high</span><span class="a-val">−100</span></div><div class="smt-hint">Measured against the previous swing high at 12 Aug 17:18 UTC. ES has since traded through it, so this is a record of what the quartet did, not a condition still in force.</div></div><div class="smt-blk"><div class="smt-hd"><div class="smt-kind">Last swing low</div><span class="smt-when">13 Aug 06:12 UTC · 337.8h ago · ES 7776.00</span><span class="smt-chip conf">ALL FOUR AGREE</span><span class="smt-chip gone">TRADED THROUGH</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>ES</b><span class="a-dir up">▲</span><span class="a-note">higher low <span style="opacity:.7">(the anchor)</span></span><span class="a-val">+11.00</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>NQ</b><span class="a-dir up">▲</span><span class="a-note">higher low</span><span class="a-val">+48.00</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>RTY</b><span class="a-dir up">▲</span><span class="a-note">higher low</span><span class="a-val">+4.20</span></div><div class="smt-arm"><b>YM</b><span class="a-dir up">▲</span><span class="a-note">higher low</span><span class="a-val">+23</span></div><div class="smt-hint">Measured against the previous swing low at 12 Aug 20:06 UTC. ES has since traded through it, so this is a record of what the quartet did, not a condition still in force.</div></div></div>
    <div class="smt-leg" id="smtLegend"><span><i class="conf"></i>all four agree</span><span><i class="one"></i>one arm diverged</span><span><i class="split"></i>the quartet split</span><u>Colour here means <b>agreement</b>, never direction — four contracts can agree on a fall and that is still green. Direction is the <span style="color:var(--up)">▲</span><span style="color:var(--down)">▼</span> beside each contract.</u></div>
    <div class="smt-blk" id="smtHistBlk">
      <div class="smt-hd"><div class="smt-kind">Recent legs</div>
        <span class="smt-when" id="smtHistN">newest first · 10 of 139 in the window</span></div>
      <div id="smtHist"><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▼</span><span class="h-when">13 Aug 06:12</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−2.2×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▲</span><span class="h-when">13 Aug 01:00</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+1.8×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▼</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 20:06</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−3.5×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▲</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 17:18</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+1.7×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▼</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 16:42</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−0.3×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▼</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 15:06</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><b style="color:var(--text-primary)">turned</b> <span style="opacity:.75">+1.7×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▲</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 14:54</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+1.0×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▼</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 14:18</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class="on one"></i><i class=""></i></span><span class="h-out"><span>carried on</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−5.7×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▲</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 14:00</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i></span><span class="h-out"><b style="color:var(--text-primary)">turned</b> <span style="opacity:.75">−0.8×</span></span></div><div class="smt-row"><span class="h-kind">▲</span><span class="h-when">12 Aug 13:30</span><span class="smt-dots"><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i><i class="on conf"></i></span><span class="h-out"><b style="color:var(--text-primary)">turned</b> <span style="opacity:.75">−2.7×</span></span></div></div>
      <div class="smt-hint" id="smtHistHint">Each row is one leg: the swing named, how many of the four made the same kind of swing, and what ES did over the <b>hour beginning three rows after</b> the pivot — never from the pivot itself, which is lower than the three rows after it <i>by definition</i> and would hand this column a beautiful number nobody could have traded. The multiple is of a typical six-minute move.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="advis" id="smtAdv"><span class="advis-hdr">What the quartet is saying</span>The last completed swing low was <b>337.8h</b> ago and <b>all four</b> made the same kind of swing: all four a higher low. Price has since traded through the level, which settles it either way. The last swing high before that, 342.9h back, had one arm out. <div class="subnote">Read this card as <b>breadth</b> — is the move carried by all four index futures or by three — and not as a trigger. The 148-hour study behind the disclosure looked for a reversal edge in divergence and did not find one, and this card publishes that result rather than burying it.</div></div>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="smtSum"><span>Divergence did not predict reversal — over 336h its best gap sits inside its own noise.</span></span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="smtBody"><p><b>What was measured.</b> Every swing high and low in 336 hours of 3,225 archived rows at a 6-minute cadence, over 12 symbol pairs that passed the synchrony test further down, both ways round. For each one: did the partner make the same kind of swing, and did price reverse afterwards. The forward window opens <b>3 rows after the pivot</b> and runs 10 rows.</p><p><b>The pooled result.</b> Share that reversed — fell after a high, rose after a low — with the gap against the base rate beside it. <span style="opacity:.7">Nothing in this section is coloured: on this card green and amber and red mean agreement, and a table where they meant “above the base rate” instead would spend that meaning twice.</span></p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>swing highs</th><th>30m</th><th>60m</th><th>120m</th></tr><tr><td>every live row</td><td>39%</td><td>37%</td><td>34%</td></tr><tr><td>all four agreed</td><td>47% <span style="opacity:.55">n219</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+8</span></td><td>41% <span style="opacity:.55">n215</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+4</span></td><td>33% <span style="opacity:.55">n168</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−1</span></td></tr><tr><td>one or more diverged</td><td>43% <span style="opacity:.55">n76</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+4</span></td><td>48% <span style="opacity:.55">n75</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+11</span></td><td>42% <span style="opacity:.55">n55</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+8</span></td></tr></tbody></table><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>swing lows</th><th>30m</th><th>60m</th><th>120m</th></tr><tr><td>every live row</td><td>60%</td><td>62%</td><td>65%</td></tr><tr><td>all four agreed</td><td>67% <span style="opacity:.55">n207</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+7</span></td><td>63% <span style="opacity:.55">n190</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+1</span></td><td>68% <span style="opacity:.55">n174</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+3</span></td></tr><tr><td>one or more diverged</td><td>58% <span style="opacity:.55">n88</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−2</span></td><td>55% <span style="opacity:.55">n76</span> <span style="opacity:.75">−7</span></td><td>67% <span style="opacity:.55">n64</span> <span style="opacity:.75">+2</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Why that is not an edge.</b> Divergent swings did turn a little more often than a random row, and so did confirmed ones — which is the tell: most of the gap belongs to <i>being at a swing at all</i>, not to diverging. The two bases are also not mirror images. Over this window a live row rose 62% of the time and fell 37%, because the window trended; reading the high rows against the rising base would manufacture a large edge out of nothing but the drift.</p><p><b>And the n are smaller than they look.</b> Each swing is counted once per usable pair, so one ES high that diverged against three partners lands in that column three times — no honest standard error can be computed from it. The saved run also prints a p-value; it is an exact binomial <b>against a fair coin</b>, which is not the question this card asks, so it is not quoted here.</p><p><b>This board’s own track record, which disagrees.</b></p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>this board’s own legs</th><th>turned</th><th>of</th></tr><tr><td>swing highs, all four agreed</td><td>32%</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>swing highs, one or more diverged</td><td>41%</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>swing lows, all four agreed</td><td>50%</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>swing lows, one or more diverged</td><td>55%</td><td>38</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Those are ES-anchored legs on the four arms this card actually draws, so unlike the pooled table each one is a distinct swing. They look far better than the study does. <b>Believe the study.</b> Six or eight legs is a sample in which a coin comes up two-thirds heads about a third of the time, and the pooled window is the same market measured hundreds of ways. A small sample that flatters the card is the single most reliable way this page could start lying to you, and it is on the card so that it cannot do it quietly.</p><p><b>Why only four contracts.</b> A divergence measured across two feeds with different delays is mostly the delay. Each symbol here is served at its own age, and the four index futures land within 59 seconds of each other — a sixth of a row — so they are genuinely simultaneous and need no correction. Everything else needed a shift, found two ways: by the delay table, and by the lag that maximises correlation. Pairs where the two methods disagreed are <b>dropped, not reconciled</b>, because a shift chosen to maximise a correlation is no longer independent evidence for it.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>pair</th><th>shift</th><th>r</th><th>used</th></tr><tr><td>DXY · GOLD</td><td>0</td><td>−0.43</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · CRUDE</td><td>-29</td><td>−0.05</td><td><span style="opacity:.5">dropped</span></td></tr><tr><td>ES · DXY</td><td>0</td><td>−0.20</td><td><span style="opacity:.5">dropped</span></td></tr><tr><td>ES · GOLD</td><td>0</td><td>+0.35</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · HSI</td><td>+1</td><td>+0.05</td><td><span style="opacity:.5">dropped</span></td></tr><tr><td>ES · NQ</td><td>0</td><td>+0.87</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · RTY</td><td>0</td><td>+0.72</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · SPX</td><td>-2</td><td>+0.45</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · US10Y</td><td>+1</td><td>−0.20</td><td><span style="opacity:.5">dropped</span></td></tr><tr><td>ES · VIX</td><td>-2</td><td>−0.31</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>ES · YM</td><td>0</td><td>+0.65</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>NQ · NDX</td><td>-1</td><td>+0.35</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>NQ · RTY</td><td>0</td><td>+0.62</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>NQ · VIX</td><td>-2</td><td>−0.25</td><td><span style="opacity:.5">dropped</span></td></tr><tr><td>NQ · YM</td><td>0</td><td>+0.38</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>RTY · RUT</td><td>+1</td><td>+0.62</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>RTY · YM</td><td>0</td><td>+0.57</td><td>yes</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Measured provider age</b>, 10th percentile of each symbol’s own stamp against its row — the 10th and not the median because the distribution is bimodal and a median describes the overnight rather than the feed: CRUDE -9747s · DXY 602s · ES 602s · GOLD 603s · HSI 909s · NDX 181s · NQ 602s · RTY 661s · RUT 962s · SPX 2s · US10Y 1081s · VIX 1s · YM 661s.</p><p><b>Why the forward window opens late.</b> Rows 1 to 3 after a swing high are lower than it <i>by construction</i> — that is what the word means — so measuring from the pivot returns a large number that restates the definition and passes any significance test you point at it. It is also the first moment the signal exists: a pivot is not known to be one until its right shoulder has printed. The late open is not a statistical nicety, it is the only version anybody could have traded.</p><p><b>Where ES sits in this.</b> ES is the clock, not the truth. Anchoring means ES can never be the arm that diverges, and a card anchored on NQ would name a different odd one out on some of the same legs. The anchor was chosen because it is what the study measured, and because the rest of this page is quoted in ES.</p></div>
    </details>
    <button class="mini" type="button" id="smtTvBtn" style="margin-top:12px">Show table view</button>
    <div class="tvwrap" id="smtTvWrap"><table class="tvbox" id="smtTv"><tbody><tr><th>Swing</th><th>UTC</th><th>Agree</th><th>ES</th><th>NQ</th><th>RTY</th><th>YM</th><th>After</th></tr><tr><td>low</td><td>13 Aug 06:12</td><td>4/4</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>carried on −2.2×</td></tr><tr><td>high</td><td>13 Aug 01:00</td><td>3/4</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>up</td><td>down</td><td>carried on +1.8×</td></tr><tr><td>low</td><td>12 Aug 20:06</td><td>3/4</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>up</td><td>down</td><td>carried on −3.5×</td></tr><tr><td>high</td><td>12 Aug 17:18</td><td>3/4</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>down</td><td>carried on +1.7×</td></tr><tr><td>low</td><td>12 Aug 16:42</td><td>3/4</td><td>up</td><td>down</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>carried on −0.3×</td></tr><tr><td>low</td><td>12 Aug 15:06</td><td>3/4</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>up</td><td>turned +1.7×</td></tr><tr><td>high</td><td>12 Aug 14:54</td><td>4/4</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>carried on +1.0×</td></tr><tr><td>low</td><td>12 Aug 14:18</td><td>3/4</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>down</td><td>carried on −5.7×</td></tr><tr><td>high</td><td>12 Aug 14:00</td><td>4/4</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>down</td><td>turned −0.8×</td></tr><tr><td>high</td><td>12 Aug 13:30</td><td>4/4</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>up</td><td>turned −2.7×</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DEPTH BOARD -->
<!-- data-feeds is NQ,ES,RTY and deliberately NOT HSI. The attribute names the
     symbols a card RENDERS FROM LIVE, and the only live number on this one is
     the current quote for whichever tab is open. The HSI panel below is a fixed
     measurement over the archive window -- it says why there is no HSI board,
     which is as true at 4am with the Hang Seng shut as at noon with it open.
     Listing HSI here hung a "closed · 13h" tag on a card that does not read the
     HSI feed at all, which is the crying-wolf failure paintCardHealth's own
     comment says the continuous/session split exists to prevent. -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="NQ,ES,RTY" id="rail-where-price-has-stood">
  <div class="card-title-anchor" id="tapCard"></div>
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Where price has stood</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>The liquidity map below is built from traded volume, and the relay banks bars for <b>ES and RTY only</b> — so NQ has never had one. </summary><div class="fwork-b">This card is the substitute: <b>time at price</b>, read off the six-minute quote tape, which is the one depth statistic that exists for every contract. It is a proxy for the volume profile and the disclosure below measures how bad a proxy it is. Shelves are where the contract stood; the gaps between them are prices it passed through without settling. Whether those gaps are <b>fast</b> is a separate claim, tested per symbol, and it does not hold everywhere.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="ctl-row">
    <div class="tabs" id="tapTabs" role="tablist"><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true">NQ</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">ES</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">RTY</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">YM</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">MHI</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div id="tapBody"><div class="tap-verdict"><span class="tap-pill weak">GAPS ARE FAST: WEAK</span><span class="tap-ratio">step inside a gap is <b>1.16×</b> the step inside a shelf <span style="opacity:.7">(11 vs 10, n=182/4094)</span></span></div><div class="tap-wrap"><div class="tap-rail" style="height:320px"><div class="tap-band sh" style="top:183.2px;height:27.4px;width:7.4%"></div><div class="tap-band sh" style="top:41.0px;height:101.2px;width:96.0%"></div><div class="tap-band sh" style="top:2.7px;height:30.1px;width:16.4%"></div><div class="tap-band vd" style="top:142.2px;height:41.0px"></div><div class="tap-band vd" style="top:32.8px;height:8.2px"></div><div class="tap-band tl" style="top:210.6px;height:109.4px"></div><div class="tap-px" style="top:92.7px"></div><div class="tap-lab sh" style="top:-1.2px"><b>30050–30325</b>12.7% of the time<br>5 visits</div><div class="tap-lab vd" style="top:38.8px"><b>29975–30050</b>0.4% · gap<br>4/10 crossed</div><div class="tap-lab sh" style="top:78.8px"><b>29050–29975</b>74.3% of the time<br>14 visits</div><div class="tap-pxt" style="top:131.8px">29,503 now</div><div class="tap-lab vd" style="top:158.8px"><b>28675–29050</b>3.7% · gap<br>1/11 crossed</div><div class="tap-lab sh" style="top:198.8px"><b>28425–28675</b>5.7% of the time<br>13 visits</div><div class="tap-lab tl" style="top:246.3px"><b>27425–28425</b>3.1% · tail<br>left 570h ago</div></div></div><p class="tap-say">Price is inside the 29,050–29,975 shelf, which has held <b>74.3%</b> of the window’s moving time across 14 separate visits. The nearest gap above is 29,975–30,050, <b>472</b> away — entered 10 times, crossed 4, median 12m spent inside. The nearest gap below is 28,675–29,050, <b>453</b> away — entered 11 times, crossed 1, median 15h spent inside.  Below <b>28,425</b> there is neither shelf nor gap: the model only classifies the range its shelves span, and this lower tail is 34% of the rail holding <b>3.1%</b> of the window’s moving time, last touched <b>570h</b> ago. Empty on the chart, but it is ground NQ has left rather than air it has never entered. On NQ the speed claim is <b>weak</b> — 1.16×, on 182 steps. Real, but not enough to size a trade around; read these as regions the contract has avoided, not as slides.</p><div class="tap-meta">Built from <b>6580</b> quotes over <b>671.5 hours</b> at a 6-minute cadence, binned at 25 points. <b>33%</b> of intervals were a repeat of the previous quote and are excluded — including them would map the hours the exchange was shut rather than the prices it traded. Median six-minute step over the whole window: 10.</div></div>
    <div class="advis" id="tapAdv"><details class="disc"><summary><span>How to use this — shelves transfer, speed does not</span></summary><div class="disc-body">The shelves are the honest half and they are honest on all three contracts: a shelf is where the contract has actually stood, and price returning to one has historically found something to do there. <b>The speed half is not transferable</b> — it is marginal on NQ at 1.16× (182 steps), is absent on YM at 1.00× (47 steps), is absent on HSI at 0.85× (16 steps). </div></details></div>
    <div class="tap-hsi" id="tapHsi"><div class="tap-hd">What Hong Kong does with Wall Street’s move</div><div class="tap-verdict"><span class="tap-pill fail">GAPS: MIXED</span><span class="tap-ratio"><b>3 of 10</b> weekday gaps were extended · 5 gave it back, 2 held it flat</span></div><table class="og-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>HK session</th><th>ES o/night</th><th>HSI gap</th><th>took</th><th>then o→c</th><th>close/close</th></tr><tr><td>07-31</td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.27%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocf" style="color:var(--text-muted)">+0.22%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.05%</b></td></tr><tr class="we"><td>08-03 <span style="font-size:9px">wknd 65.5h</span></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.66%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.37%</b></td><td><b>56%</b></td><td><b class="ocf" style="color:var(--text-muted)">−0.09%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.29%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-04</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+1.13%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.49%</b></td><td><b>43%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−1.09%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.78%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-05</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+1.77%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.14%</b></td><td><b>8%</b></td><td><b class="ocf" style="color:var(--text-muted)">−0.01%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.14%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-06</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.52%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.39%</b></td><td><b>266%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−0.73%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.65%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-07</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.27%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.41%</b></td><td><b>152%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.40%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.38%</b></td></tr><tr class="we"><td>08-10 <span style="font-size:9px">wknd 65.4h</span></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.42%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.37%</b></td><td><b>90%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.48%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+1.02%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-11</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.09%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.17%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−1.29%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.05%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-12</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.33%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.89%</b></td><td><b>268%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−0.38%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.98%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-13</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.05%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.36%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.34%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.26%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-14</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.55%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.78%</b></td><td><b>-141%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−0.39%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.08%</b></td></tr><tr class="we"><td>08-17 <span style="font-size:9px">wknd 65.4h</span></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.15%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.99%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.74%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+1.49%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-18</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.66%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.17%</b></td><td><b>25%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.51%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.18%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-19</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.07%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.45%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.51%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.02%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-20</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.28%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.94%</b></td><td><b>338%</b></td><td><b class="ocf" style="color:var(--text-muted)">−0.18%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.97%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-21</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.87%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.40%</b></td><td><b>-46%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--good)">+0.63%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.95%</b></td></tr><tr class="we"><td>08-24 <span style="font-size:9px">wknd 65.4h</span></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.22%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.30%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−1.09%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−1.96%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-25</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.02%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.22%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−0.52%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.06%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-26</td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.12%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.71%</b></td><td><span style="color:var(--text-muted)">–</span></td><td><b class="ocf" style="color:var(--text-muted)">+0.09%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.57%</b></td></tr><tr><td>08-27</td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.47%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--good)">+0.21%</b></td><td><b>45%</b></td><td><b class="ocd" style="color:var(--critical)">−0.88%</b></td><td><b style="color:var(--critical)">−0.41%</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="tap-say">Of <b>10</b> weekday windows, <b>3</b> added to the gap, 5 gave it back and 2 held it flat. The share is <b>not</b> ordered by the size of the move: +1.77% handed over 8% and −0.27% handed over 152%.</p><details class="fwork tap-f" data-t="break"><summary><b>What would break it</b> — 10 windows is the whole weekday sample, and one that imports 90% and holds it kills the pattern</summary><div class="fwork-b"><p class="tap-say">The archive is 27 days old and two of those days are a weekend, one is uncovered and one begins mid-session, which is why <b>10</b> clean weekday windows is the whole of it. A window that imports 90% of an overnight move and holds it to the close kills the pattern outright, and nothing here would survive that. The weekend row is greyed because it spans 65.5 hours and two US sessions, so its ratio is not the same measurement. The gap is read at HSI’s first NEW print, <b>3 minutes after the bell</b>, because the 09:30 sample is the prior close held on every day in this archive; a real opening auction print would move these numbers and the relay does not carry one. ES is delay-corrected by the measured ten minutes. HSI cannot be, and is not.</p></div></details><details class="fwork tap-f" data-t="noboard"><summary><b>Why HSI has no shelf board</b> — 65 prints a session, and the feed carries <b>no volume at all</b></summary><div class="fwork-b"><p class="tap-say">Over the same 671.5 hours the relay published <b>6580</b> HSI quotes carrying <b>1171</b> price changes, and <b>83%</b> of consecutive quotes are a repeat. <b>That number is not about the feed.</b> 80% of those samples were taken while the Hong Kong exchange was shut, and across the shut hours the repeat rate is <b>98%</b> because there is nothing for the quote to do. Inside the weekday cash session it is <b>15%</b>, and with the 12:00–13:00 lunch halt taken out, <b>5%</b>. During the hours it trades, this quote moves on 95 of every 100 consecutive samples. It is a live tape.</p><p class="tap-say">The longest unbroken run of identical quotes in the archive is <b>46h</b> — and it runs Saturday lunchtime to Monday morning, so it is <b>the weekend</b>, not a stall. Restricted to hours the exchange was open, the longest run is 54m, and that one straddles the lunch halt; take the halt out and the longest the price ever stood still while Hong Kong was trading is <b>12m</b>.</p><p class="tap-say">So the board is refused — but not for the reason this card used to give. It is refused because <b>20 cash sessions</b> have yielded 1293 in-session samples between them, roughly 65 a day at one every 6 minutes, and because <b>the feed carries no volume at all</b>. The whole range was 4.14% against ES’s 6.38%, and every change landed in UTC hours 1–8 — the Hong Kong cash session and nothing else.</p><div class="tap-strip"><div class="tap-day nc">07-30<b>–</b>no cash</div><div class="tap-day">07-31<b>21</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-01<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-02<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day">08-03<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-04<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-05<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-06<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-07<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-08<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-09<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day">08-10<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-11<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-12<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-13<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-14<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-15<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-16<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day">08-17<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-18<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-19<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-20<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-21<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-22<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day frz">08-23<b>0</b>shut</div><div class="tap-day">08-24<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-25<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-26<b>55</b>moves</div><div class="tap-day">08-27<b>53</b>moves</div></div><p class="tap-say" style="color:var(--text-muted)">A shelf on the other three boards is volume: contracts actually traded at a price. A shelf drawn from this feed would be time, inferred from about sixty prints a session — and the two answer different questions. <b>Drawing the second and labelling it the first is the error this card exists to refuse</b>, and it would look exactly as confident as the real ones. Red days are ones the quote never moved through — two of them are a weekend, which is the feed holding its last print rather than a quiet market. Grey days are ones the archive does not cover at all: 30 July begins after the Hong Kong close and 6 August has not reached the bell. <b>Not covered and not moving are different facts</b> and this strip used to print the first as the second.</p></div></details></div>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="tapSum">How good a proxy is a clock for a volume profile — <b>rough</b>: value areas overlap 74%, rank correlation 0.80</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="tapCal"><p>ES is the only contract on this page with <b>both</b> a volume profile and a time profile, so it is the only place the substitution can be checked. It was, over the same window and the same 5-point bins, and the answer is that the clock is a <b>rough</b> stand-in for volume — good enough to locate a shelf, not good enough to quote a level off.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>From the clock</th><th>From volume</th></tr><tr><td>Value area (70%)</td><td>7,670–7,795</td><td>7,680–7,825</td></tr><tr><td>… they overlap</td><td colspan="2">74%</td></tr><tr><td>Mean price</td><td>7,717.5</td><td>7,699.0</td></tr><tr><td>Worst single bin</td><td colspan="2">1.43 points of share</td></tr><tr><td>Rank correlation</td><td colspan="2">0.797 over all 95 bins · 0.667 over the heaviest 24</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>The bias has a direction and it is structural.</b> The clock’s mean sits 18.5 points <i>above</i> volume’s on a window that drifted 345.75 points. A clock banks time wherever price is standing now, including overnight on thin flow; volume stays behind at the levels the cash sessions actually worked. In a trending window the time profile therefore leans toward the recent end, and every shelf on this card should be read with that lean in mind.</p><p><b>Removing stillness is what makes it usable at all.</b> Raw, the worst bin was off by 6.24 points of share; with repeated quotes dropped that falls to 1.43. The rank correlation barely moves (0.776 → 0.797), which is the honest reading of the fix: it removes a gross artefact and buys no bin-level fidelity.</p><p><b>No point of control is quoted anywhere on this card</b>, and the reason is not the proxy. Swept across bin widths the <i>volume</i> POC itself moves 7,774 → 7,773 → 7,775 → 7,770 (bins 2.5 to 20), a range of 4 points. A single most-traded price is the wrong statistic for this window regardless of how it is measured, so the card publishes runs and not peaks.</p><p><b>And the speed test survives its own bin width.</b> The ratio at the top of the card is one cut of the bins; a result that only holds at one cut is a result about the cut. Every contract was re-measured at six widths, with the void sample size carried, because at a wide enough bin the gaps swallow almost nothing and the ratio goes wherever two observations send it.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Bins</th><th>NQ</th><th>ES</th><th>RTY</th><th>YM</th><th>HSI</th></tr><tr><td>widest</td><td>1.18× <span style="opacity:.55">n36</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@50</span></td><td>1.40× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n12</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@2</span></td><td>2.00× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n5</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@4</span></td><td>2.50× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n22</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@37.5</span></td><td>0.85× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n16</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@10</span></td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>1.13× <span style="opacity:.55">n39</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@37.5</span></td><td>—</td><td>2.00× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n21</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@3</span></td><td>1.00× <span style="opacity:.55">n47</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@25</span></td><td>0.85× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n21</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@8</span></td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>1.16× <span style="opacity:.55">n182</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@25</span></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>1.09× <span style="opacity:.55">n103</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@5</span></td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>1.54× <span style="opacity:.9;color:var(--critical)">n25</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@20</span></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>0.88× <span style="opacity:.55">n179</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@4</span></td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>0.82× <span style="opacity:.55">n150</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@12.5</span></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>narrowest</td><td>0.82× <span style="opacity:.55">n171</span> <span style="opacity:.45">@10</span></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="color:var(--text-muted)">Red sample sizes are below thirty void steps and should be ignored — they are where NQ’s ratio finally exceeds 1, and they are the reason it is reported as failing rather than as marginal.</p></div>
    </details>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="tapXSum">Every gap price has entered, and how it left — <b>5 of 21</b> crossed on NQ</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="tapX"><p>The last 8 of <b>21</b> episodes on NQ, newest last. <b>5</b> of the 21 ended on the far side.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>Entered</th><th>Gap</th><th>From</th><th>Inside</th><th>Left</th></tr><tr><td>08-05 14:08Z</td><td>29,975–30,050</td><td>below</td><td>12m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-10 09:26Z</td><td>29,975–30,050</td><td>below</td><td>6m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-13 13:38Z</td><td>29,975–30,050</td><td>below</td><td>12m</td><td><span class="disc-yes">crossed</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-14 15:38Z</td><td>29,975–30,050</td><td>above</td><td>18m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-18 01:50Z</td><td>29,975–30,050</td><td>above</td><td>24m</td><td><span class="disc-yes">crossed</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-24 13:44Z</td><td>28,675–29,050</td><td>above</td><td>54m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-24 15:08Z</td><td>28,675–29,050</td><td>above</td><td>6m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr><tr><td>08-25 00:20Z</td><td>28,675–29,050</td><td>above</td><td>6m</td><td><span class="disc-no">turned back</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    </details>
