</section>

<!-- MACRO -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="VIX,HSI,CRUDE,GOLD,DXY" id="rail-macro-volatility">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Macro &amp; volatility</div>
    <div class="card-sub">The slower channel. VIX arrives near-live via CNBC; the rest carries the same delay as the futures.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body"><div id="macroList"><div class="mrow"><div class="nm">VIX<span>volatility, near-live · 15s old · no change figure: published minus 5.91% off a prev_close of 17.09 while this page’s own 31 Jul news entry records Friday’s close as 15.99 — from 15.99 the same px of 16.07 is plus 0.50%, so the sign was wrong, not just the size; the live payload now carries 15.99 correctly, which is why one payload cannot settle it</span></div><div class="pv">14.91</div><div class="cv" style="color:var(--text-muted)">—</div></div><div class="mrow"><div class="nm">HSI<span>Hang Seng · 17m old · no change figure: prev_close froze after Thursday, thawed wrong — this payload is on the yahoo path and still publishes 25858.88 against a true Friday close of 25884.43, so the defect outlived the vendor swap and is structural; freeze/thaw is unobservable from one payload</span></div><div class="pv">25,557.89</div><div class="cv" style="color:var(--text-muted)">—</div></div><div class="mrow"><div class="nm">Crude<span>WTI front month · 10m old · no change figure: two Friday closes published — 86.80 held for 31h across Saturday, then replaced by 84.67 at 04:00Z Sunday, before any reopen; seen on the cnbc path, source now yahoo</span></div><div class="pv">81.06</div><div class="cv" style="color:var(--text-muted)">—</div></div><div class="mrow"><div class="nm">Gold<span>front month · 12m old</span></div><div class="pv">4,651.20</div><div class="cv" style="color:var(--up)">+1.15%</div></div><div class="mrow"><div class="nm">DXY<span>dollar index · 12m old</span></div><div class="pv">99.15</div><div class="cv" style="color:var(--down)">-0.02%</div></div></div></div>
</section>

<!-- THE DOLLAR -->
<!-- data-feeds is DXY alone. Every other symbol on this card appears inside a
     BAKED measurement over the archive window -- the correlation grid, the
     conditional base rate, the day strip -- and a baked number does not go
     stale when a feed does. The only live thing here is the dollar's own
     quote. Listing ES or GOLD would dim this card whenever the futures gapped,
     for numbers that were computed hours ago and are not affected. -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="DXY" id="rail-the-dollar">
  <div class="card-title-anchor" id="fxCard"></div>
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">The dollar</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>Three cards on this page use the dollar to explain something. </summary><div class="fwork-b">This one checks whether it can. Over <b>148 hours</b> of six-minute rows the dollar’s tie to US index futures is weak and <b>fading</b>, and the one thing it does reliably predict is not a direction — it is a <b>size</b>. The yen, the intervention and the Bank of Japan are underneath, kept separate and hand-written, because the relay banks no FX pairs at all and none of that is measurable here.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div id="fxHero"><div class="smt-when smt-gen">measured 13 Aug 08:24 UTC · 13 days ago <span class="dim">as measured</span> <span class="dim">· carried forward: the last refresh could not regenerate this card</span></div><div style="margin-bottom:9px"><span class="fx-chip warn"><i>▲</i>volatility, not direction</span></div><div class="fx-hero"><div class="fx-cell"><div class="k">ES up next 30m<br>on any half hour</div><div class="v">55%</div><div class="n">median move <b>0.039%</b><br>2000 overlapping windows</div></div><div class="fx-cell hi"><div class="k">ES up next 30m<br>after a big dollar move</div><div class="v">51%</div><div class="n">median move <b>0.064%</b> (+62% wider)<br>top decile, n=200</div></div></div></div>
    <div id="fxLvl"><div class="fx-lvl"><span class="px">99.977</span><span class="sym">DXY at the close of the window</span><span class="ch dn">−1.10% over 336.0h</span><span class="rng">range 99.41–100.99</span></div><div class="subnote">Since then the dollar is <b>−0.83%</b> at 99.147 (last snapshot), which the measurements below do not include.</div></div>
    <div class="fx-days" id="fxDays"><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Thu 30</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:50.0%"></div></div><div class="p dn">−0.98%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Fri 31</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:22.4%"></div></div><div class="p dn">−0.44%</div></div><div class="fx-day thin"><div class="d">Sat 1</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:0.0%"></div></div><div class="p up">0.00%</div></div><div class="fx-day thin"><div class="d">Sun 2</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:7.7%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.15%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Mon 3</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:11.1%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.22%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Tue 4</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:5.5%"></div></div><div class="p dn">−0.11%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Wed 5</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:7.7%"></div></div><div class="p dn">−0.15%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Thu 6</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:12.7%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.25%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Fri 7</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:18.6%"></div></div><div class="p dn">−0.37%</div></div><div class="fx-day thin"><div class="d">Sat 8</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:0.0%"></div></div><div class="p up">0.00%</div></div><div class="fx-day thin"><div class="d">Sun 9</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:0.8%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.02%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Mon 10</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:8.8%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.17%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Tue 11</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:0.0%"></div></div><div class="p up">0.00%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Wed 12</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:6.5%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.13%</div></div><div class="fx-day"><div class="d">Thu 13</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:0.8%"></div></div><div class="p up">+0.01%</div></div><div class="subnote"><b>Thu 30</b> alone is −0.98% of the window’s −1.10% — <b>89%</b> of the whole move in one session. “The dollar fell all week” and “the dollar fell in one afternoon and then stopped” are different facts, and only the second one is true here.</div></div>
    <div class="fx-cor" id="fxCor"><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">ES</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:20.4%"></div></div><div class="r dn">−0.20</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip good">stable</span><span class="hh">−0.18 → −0.26</span></div></div><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">NQ</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:17.3%"></div></div><div class="r dn">−0.17</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip good">stable</span><span class="hh">−0.14 → −0.25</span></div></div><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">RTY</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:13.3%"></div></div><div class="r dn">−0.13</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip none">too small</span><span class="hh">−0.07 → −0.28</span></div></div><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">GOLD</div><div class="track"><div class="bar dn" style="right:50%;width:44.1%"></div></div><div class="r dn">−0.44</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip good">stable</span><span class="hh">−0.38 → −0.56</span></div></div><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">VIX</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:4.6%"></div></div><div class="r up">+0.05</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip none">too small</span><span class="hh">+0.06 → −0.00</span></div></div><div class="fx-crow"><div class="s">US10Y</div><div class="track"><div class="bar up" style="left:50%;width:4.6%"></div></div><div class="r up">+0.05</div><div class="v"><span class="fx-chip none">too small</span><span class="hh">+0.03 → +0.10</span></div></div></div>
    <div class="fx-halves" id="fxHalves">Bar and number are the correlation of six-minute moves against the dollar’s: a bar to the left means it moved <b>opposite</b> to the dollar. The pill answers a different question — whether the window’s two halves agreed — and the pair beneath each pill is those two halves.<details class="fwork fx-lg"><summary><b>TOO SMALL</b> is about size, not agreement · floors |r| 0.15 to report, 0.40 to trade · measured on <b>2164</b> of 3224 steps</summary><div class="fwork-b fx-lgb"><p>Under |r| = 0.15 the number is not called a relationship at all, however steadily it holds — which is why a row can read <b>TOO SMALL</b> above two halves that plainly agree with each other. They do agree. There is just not enough of it to be worth a verdict. Trading the direction takes more again: the advisory below uses |r| = 0.40. Both floors are judged rather than measured, which is why they are printed.</p><p>A step counts only where both series printed, so 1060 of the window’s 3224 six-minute steps are missing one side and score nothing. Feed delays differ, so VIX shifted 2 rows, 119s unresolved and US10Y shifted -1 row, 119s unresolved. A correlation measured across an unshifted delay is mostly the delay, which is why the shift is applied and the seconds it cannot fix are printed rather than rounded away.</p></div></details></div>
    <div class="advis" id="fxAdv"><span class="advis-hdr">What to do with the dollar today</span><details class="fwork adv-i" data-t="adv:dir"><summary><span class="t">Do not trade the direction.</span><span class="n">ES −0.20 · r² 4%</span></summary><div class="fwork-b adv-b">Against ES the dollar reads <b>−0.20</b> over the whole window and <b>−0.26</b> over its second half — not a tie worth acting on, whatever the pill above says about its stability. A steady relationship and a useful one are different claims, and only the second one is a reason to trade. Three other cards on this page use the dollar to explain an equity move; on this window that explanation carries about <b>4%</b> of the variance, and less than that lately. The line between the two readings above is |r| = 0.40, which is judged and not measured.</div></details><details class="fwork adv-i" data-t="adv:size"><summary><span class="t">Trade the size instead.</span><span class="n">range +62%</span></summary><div class="fwork-b adv-b">On an ordinary half hour ES rose <b>55%</b> of the time and moved 0.039% in absolute terms. In the half hour after the dollar’s largest moves — the top decile, anything past 0.065% in thirty minutes — it rose <b>51%</b> of the time and moved 0.064%. The direction edge <b>vanishes</b> to a coin flip and the range grows by about <b>62%</b>. It does not matter which way the dollar went: after a fall ES rose 48% of the time, after a rise 55%. So a violent dollar is a <b>volatility</b> signal and specifically not a direction one — widen stops, expect the next half hour to be bigger, and take the direction from something else.</div></details><details class="fwork adv-i" data-t="adv:gold"><summary><span class="t">Gold is the one dependable pair.</span><span class="n">−0.44 · both halves</span></summary><div class="fwork-b adv-b">At <b>−0.44</b> and holding across both halves. It is also contemporaneous: gold is a mirror the dollar is already in, not a window onto the next half hour. Useful for confirming that a dollar move is real rather than a feed artefact, which on this archive is a live question.</div></details></div>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="fxGapSum">2 rows out of 2166 set the sign of this card</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="fxGap"><p>The archive steps every six minutes, and this card’s correlations are correlations of six-minute moves. But the relay does not always step six minutes: it stalls, and it goes down overnight. A row pair spanning one of those holes is still a “step” to any code that walks the array, and it carries a whole session’s move.</p><p>There are <b>2</b> such rows in this window, out of 2166 the market filter otherwise accepts:</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>step</th><th>spans</th><th>DXY</th><th>ES</th></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 16:12</td><td>18m</td><td>−0.045%</td><td>+0.185%</td></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 16:30</td><td>13.5h</td><td>+0.294%</td><td>+0.890%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>They are two rows in eight hundred and thirty-five, and they set the <b>sign</b> of this card:</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>pair</th><th>excluded</th><th>included</th><th></th></tr><tr><td>DXY / ES</td><td>−0.204</td><td class="">−0.062</td><td class=""></td></tr><tr><td>DXY / NQ</td><td>−0.173</td><td class="">−0.055</td><td class=""></td></tr><tr><td>DXY / RTY</td><td>−0.133</td><td class="">−0.013</td><td class=""></td></tr><tr><td>DXY / GOLD</td><td>−0.441</td><td class="">−0.477</td><td class=""></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every number on this card is computed with them excluded. The reason this is on the face of a disclosure rather than in a comment is that <b>every other card on this page walks the same archive</b>, and an overnight hole is not a six-minute move anywhere.</p></div>
    </details>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="fxBigSum">The largest dollar moves, and what moved with them</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="fxBig"><p>The largest half-hour dollar moves in the window, on market-filtered steps only, with what the futures and gold did across the same thirty minutes. <b>Same window, not the next one</b> — these are companions, not consequences, and the hero figure above is the only forward-looking number on this card.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>when (UTC)</th><th>DXY</th><th>ES</th><th>NQ</th><th>gold</th></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 13:42</td><td>−0.619%</td><td>+0.439%</td><td>+1.076%</td><td>+0.878%</td></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 13:36</td><td>−0.478%</td><td>+0.361%</td><td>+1.043%</td><td>+0.548%</td></tr><tr><td>Fri 7 12:30</td><td>−0.457%</td><td>+0.261%</td><td>+0.631%</td><td>+1.181%</td></tr><tr><td>Fri 7 12:36</td><td>−0.441%</td><td>+0.277%</td><td>+0.614%</td><td>+1.153%</td></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 13:24</td><td>−0.436%</td><td>+0.196%</td><td>+0.441%</td><td>+0.491%</td></tr><tr><td>Fri 7 12:24</td><td>−0.413%</td><td>+0.277%</td><td>+0.470%</td><td>+1.220%</td></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 13:48</td><td>−0.407%</td><td>+0.263%</td><td>+0.802%</td><td>+0.019%</td></tr><tr><td>Thu 30 13:30</td><td>−0.399%</td><td>+0.260%</td><td>+0.752%</td><td>+0.528%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Timestamps are row labels. The futures, gold and the dollar all come off the same provider about <b>602 seconds</b> late, so the prices in a row were true that long before the label — simultaneous with each other, and earlier than they look.</p><p>The filter matters more here than anywhere else on the card. Without it the top of this table is a weekend: the dollar prints a third of a per cent at 03:18 on a Saturday and gives it straight back at 03:24, with the futures and gold at exactly zero on both steps. That is the relay, not the market, and it is the largest “dollar move” in the archive. A step counts here only if at least two of ES, NQ, RTY and gold also changed across it.</p></div>
    </details>
    <details class="disc">
      <summary><span id="fxLeadSum">One lead that looked real and is not</span></summary>
      <div class="disc-body" id="fxLead"><p>The largest number anywhere in the lead/lag grid is the dollar leading by one six-minute step. It is not on the face of this card, and this is the record of why — so that the next person to find it knows it was already found.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>DXY leads</th><th>whole</th><th>first</th><th>second</th><th>third</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>−0.130</td><td>−0.171</td><td>−0.116</td><td>−0.036</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>−0.158</td><td>−0.232</td><td>−0.113</td><td>−0.019</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>−0.065</td><td>−0.038</td><td>−0.098</td><td>−0.087</td></tr><tr><td>GOLD</td><td>+0.010</td><td>+0.045</td><td>−0.010</td><td>−0.007</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every <b>equity</b> arm is at its largest in the first third of the window and near nothing by the last. What happens in between is not a decay so much as a wander: ES collapses to zero in the middle third and then partly returns, RTY changes sign twice, and gold — which has no lead at all over the whole window — posts its largest number in the middle. A relationship does not behave like that. A single violent afternoon averaged over six days behaves exactly like that, and this window contains one: it is in the first third.</p></div>
    </details>
    <div class="fx-narr" id="fxNarr"><div class="hd"><span>The yen, and why none of it is in the numbers above</span><em>written by hand · 2026-08-05</em></div><div class="disc-body" style="padding-top:0"><p><b>The 30 July intervention worked on the yen and did nothing to the dollar.</b> The yen leg is 13.6% of DXY; across the two sessions after the United States joined Japan in supporting the yen, the yen gained <b>3.8%</b> and DXY finished net <b>+0.07%</b>.</p></div><div class="fx-tl"><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="was" data-t="What was announced, and by whom"><summary><span class="w">Background</span><span class="t">What was announced, and by whom</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">The first joint action since <b>2011</b> on one account and since <b>1998</b> on another, and the sources genuinely disagree — the disagreement is published here rather than resolved by picking whichever reads better. It was confirmed publicly on Sunday <b>3 August</b>. The dollar index fell <b>1.0%</b> on the week to a seven-week low, which is the figure most reports led with and is not the one that matters here: a week contains a great deal more than one afternoon.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="was" data-t="The intervention"><summary><span class="w">Thu 30 Jul</span><span class="t">The intervention</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">Joint US–Japan action to support the yen. Tokyo may have sold around <b>$58.97bn</b> in New York hours; the US Treasury commitment is reported at <b>$5–10bn</b>. USD/JPY had made a pre-intervention high of <b>163.99</b>.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="was" data-t="The Bank of Japan held at 1%"><summary><span class="w">Fri 31 Jul</span><span class="t">The Bank of Japan held at 1%</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">Held, but with what reporting called its <b>most explicit signal to date</b> of an early hike. Odds on a September move went to <b>44%</b> from around 30%.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="was" data-t="Confirmed"><summary><span class="w">Sun 3 Aug</span><span class="t">Confirmed</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">Both sides confirmed the action publicly. Bessent cited the “substantial undervaluation of the yen”; Trump framed it as friendship. DXY printed a seven-week low of <b>99.42</b>.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="is" data-t="Where it left the pair"><summary><span class="w">Levels now</span><span class="t">Where it left the pair</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">The pair is <b>USD/JPY</b> throughout, so <b>higher is a weaker yen</b> and the intervention being given back. It sits at <b>157.47</b>, down from a pre-intervention <b>163.99</b>: the yen up <b>3.8%</b> across two sessions, and up as much as a further 1.4% at the pair’s low of <b>155.20</b>. Floor now read at <b>155</b>, resistance <b>160</b>, Fibonacci target <b>162–163</b>. Japanese corporate hedgers are benchmarked at <b>150</b>.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="next" data-t="What re-tests it"><summary><span class="w">Ahead</span><span class="t">What re-tests it</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb"><b>US payrolls on 7 August</b> is named as the trigger that could re-test <b>160</b> — which on the convention above is most of the intervention handed back. The remaining BoJ meetings this year are <b>16–17 Sep</b>, <b>29–30 Oct</b> and <b>17–18 Dec</b>. Japan’s prior solo effort ran <b>¥11.7T ($72.52bn)</b> across April–May 2026.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e" data-k="is" data-t="The other legs of the index"><summary><span class="w">Elsewhere</span><span class="t">The other legs of the index</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb">EUR is <b>57.6%</b> of DXY against the yen’s 13.6%, and EUR/USD stabilised near <b>1.15</b> with speculative net EUR shorts at <b>$10.3bn</b> as of 28 July — the largest since February 2020. GBP is 11.9% and CAD 9.1%. Those are the legs that absorbed the yen’s move and left the index flat.</div></details><details class="fwork fx-e fx-note" data-k="none" data-t="measured"><summary><span class="w">Measured?</span><span class="t">None of the above is, and the weak correlation above it is not evidence against it</span></summary><div class="fwork-b fx-eb"><p>The tape on 30 July is CONSISTENT WITH the reported intervention and does not prove it: the largest half-hour in the whole archive is that afternoon's US cash open, and its shape — dollar down hard, gold up harder, futures up — is what a dollar-selling operation looks like. It is also what a dovish repricing looks like. This page cannot tell them apart and does not claim to. Note too that a −0.62% move in DXY is about three times what the yen leg alone can arithmetically produce.</p><p>None of the above can be measured on this page and none of it should be read as contradicted by the correlations above. The mechanism that matters — a BoJ hike unwinding the yen carry trade and forcing deleveraging into US equities — is a <b>tail event</b>. A six-minute correlation over six days cannot see one, however carefully it is computed. A weak measured tie and a live narrative risk are not in conflict; they are answers to two different questions.</p></div></details></div><div class="fx-src">Sources: <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/8/3/japan-and-us-confirm-rare-joint-intervention-to-prop-up-yen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Al Jazeera — Japan and US confirm rare joint intervention</a> · <a href="https://www.ig.com/en/news-and-trade-ideas/yen-intervention-implications-for-dxy-eur-260805" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IG — Yen intervention outlook: USD/JPY, dollar index and EUR/USD</a> · <a href="https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/usdjpy-intervention-risks-key-levels-and-scenarios-after-historic-yen-defense-93CH-4836371" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Investing.com — USD/JPY intervention risks: key levels and scenarios</a> · <a href="https://fortune.com/2026/08/03/yen-rises-amid-speculation-of-more-intervention-after-u-s-support/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fortune — Yen rises amid speculation of more intervention</a> · <a href="https://www.japantimes.co.jp/business/2026/07/31/markets/yen-intervention-breaking-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Japan Times — Yen strengthens on possible intervention</a> · <a href="https://www.mnimarkets.com/calendars/bank-of-japan-meeting-calendar" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MNI — Bank of Japan meeting calendar</a></div></div>
